// ============================================================================
// lib/result.bx — failure, made explicit.
//
//     use "lib/result.bx";
//
// A function that can fail says so in its type. There is no exception to catch
// and no error code to forget, because the compiler will not let a `Result` be
// read without saying what happens when it is an error.
//
// A LIBRARY file, like lib/option.bx: `Result<T, E>` is three lines of Burxt.
//
// It also holds the §D1o error helpers, at the foot of the file: `option_ok_or`, which turns an
// absence into a stated reason, and the four that stop the program when there is no caller who
// could act on a failure. The section there argues for why they sit next to the type that exists
// to avoid them.
// ============================================================================

// For `option_ok_or`. The dependency runs this way round and not the other: `lib/option.bx` is
// imported by every module here and stays free of imports itself.
use "option.bx";

// Either an answer or a reason there is none. `E` is usually a String while a
// program is young and an enum once its failures are worth naming.
enum Result<T, E> {
    Ok(T),
    Error(E)
}

// ---- Writing one -----------------------------------------------------------
//
//     function divide(a: Int, b: Int) -> Result<Int, String> {
//         if b == 0 {
//             return Result.Error("division by zero");
//         }
//         return Result.Ok(divide_toward_zero(a, b));
//     }
//
// ---- Reading one -----------------------------------------------------------
//
//     match divide(10, 2) {
//         Ok(answer) => { print(answer); }
//         Error(why) => { print(why); }
//     }
//
// Both arms are required. That is the whole feature.

// The answer, or the fallback. For when a failure has an obvious substitute.
pure function result_or<T, E>(r: Result<T, E>, fallback: T) -> T {
    match r {
        Ok(value) => { return value; }
        Error(why) => { return fallback; }
    }
}

pure function result_is_ok<T, E>(r: Result<T, E>) -> Bool {
    match r {
        Ok(value) => { return true; }
        Error(why) => { return false; }
    }
}

// The other half, and it is not `!result_is_ok(r)` at the call site by accident: a condition
// spelled `if result_is_error(r)` reads the way the reader is thinking, and a `!` in front of a
// predicate is the single easiest thing to miss in a diff.
pure function result_is_error<T, E>(r: Result<T, E>) -> Bool {
    match r {
        Ok(value) => { return false; }
        Error(why) => { return true; }
    }
}

// ---- adding context to a failure -------------------------------------------
//
// "no such file" is a true answer and a useless one; "reading the config: no such file" is the
// same answer with the thing that was being attempted in front of it. This is what `?` will not do
// for you — see the note at the foot of this file on why converting an error type is a decision an
// operator must not make silently — so it is spelled out, once, here.

// `context` and `": "` in front of the failure, if there is one. An `Ok` passes through untouched.
//
//     let text: String = result_context(read_config(path), "reading " + path)?;
//
// **`String` errors only, and that is a real limit rather than an oversight.** A generic `E` cannot
// be concatenated — nothing in the language says an arbitrary `E` has any text in it — and the
// alternative, an interface with a `describe()` method, would make every enum-typed failure in a
// program implement it before this function could be called on any of them. A program whose
// failures have grown into an enum has outgrown this helper and should write its own `match`, which
// is the same advice `result_or` gives.
//
// Two `+` for one call and no loop, so §D0's chunk rule does not apply — that rule is about
// building a String piece by piece in a loop, and this is a single concatenation of three pieces.
pure function result_context<T>(r: Result<T, String>, context: String) -> Result<T, String> {
    match r {
        Ok(value) => { return Result.Ok(value); }
        Error(why) => { return Result.Error(context + ": " + why); }
    }
}

// ---- the bridge from lib/option.bx -----------------------------------------

// An absence, given a reason. `Option<T>` says there is nothing there; `Result<T, E>` says why, and
// this is the one-line conversion between them.
//
//     let user: User = option_ok_or(find_user(id), "no user " + to_string(id))?;
//
// **It lives here rather than in `lib/option.bx`** because it hands back a `Result`: a caller who
// can use the answer already has this file open, and `lib/option.bx` stays the module with no
// dependencies — every other module in this library imports it.
//
// The reason is taken BY VALUE, like `option_or`'s fallback, so a caller building an expensive
// message pays for it whether or not the Option was empty. Burxt has no closures, so a lazy
// version would need a function value it does not have; `lib/fn.bx`'s interfaces could stand in,
// and the ceremony would cost more than the message.
//
// **This was listed as blocked on A3** — `Option.None` in a free generic — for long enough that the
// row went stale. A3 had been working since v0.0.241, and the fixture proves it here rather than
// the roadmap claiming it.
pure function option_ok_or<T, E>(o: Option<T>, why: E) -> Result<T, E> {
    match o {
        None => { return Result.Error(why); }
        Some(value) => { return Result.Ok(value); }
    }
}

// ============================================================================
// §D1o — WHEN THERE IS NO CALLER TO TELL
// ============================================================================
//
// Everything above this line is failure as a VALUE: the caller is handed a `Result` and the
// compiler makes them say what happens next. That is the right answer almost every time, and it is
// why `unwrap` is absent from this file and from `lib/option.bx`.
//
// The four functions below are the other case, and it is narrow: **the program's own invariant is
// broken, so there is no sensible next step and nobody who could take it.** A parser that reaches a
// branch its own grammar says is impossible has not encountered a failure — it has discovered that
// it is wrong about itself, and returning `Result.Error("impossible")` to a caller who can only
// print it is theatre.
//
// They live in this file, next to the type that exists to avoid them, ON PURPOSE. Both options are
// then in front of a reader at the same time, together with the argument for preferring the first —
// which is exactly the choice `panic` in another module, with no `Result` in view, would let
// somebody skip.
//
// **All four end the process with code 70**, the same code the runtime uses for a failed `requires`
// or an arithmetic overflow. A shell, a CI job and a test runner should not have to tell "the
// program asserted something false" apart from "the language caught something false" — both mean
// the program was wrong about itself and stopped instead of continuing. The MESSAGE distinguishes
// them: the runtime says `burxt runtime error:` and these say `burxt panic:`, so a reader knows
// which layer noticed.
//
// The message goes to **stderr**, for `lib/log.bx`'s reason: a program's stdout belongs to the
// program's output, and a dying message on it corrupts whatever is downstream of the pipe.
//
// ---- four unprefixed names, and the collision is the point ----
//
// `lib/README.md`'s rule is that every function carries its module — `string_find`, not `find` —
// because there is no namespacing and a short name would collide with a program's own. These four
// break it, the same way `math.bx`'s `INT_MAX` does and for the same reason: `panic`, `todo`,
// `unreachable` and `assert_that` are the names these things have in every language a reader has
// used, and a reader looking for them will type them.
//
// The cost is real and worth stating rather than glossing: `use "lib/result.bx"` puts `panic` and
// `todo` into a program's one flat namespace, and anything importing a module that imports this one
// gets them too. **A collision is a compile error, never a silent shadow** — which is the whole
// reason the prefix rule exists — so a program that already has its own `todo` finds out at build
// time, in one line, and either renames or wanted this one. When modules get namespaces the
// question disappears.
//
// ---- what a `requires` clause does better, and when to use one instead ----
//
// `requires parts > 0` on the function is stronger than `assert_that(parts > 0, ...)` inside it,
// and where a precondition fits it should win. The clause is part of the SIGNATURE: `burxt review`
// can see it change, `burxt mcp-schema` publishes it, and the failure names the clause and the
// function rather than a string somebody wrote. `assert_that` is for what a signature cannot say —
// a relationship between two locals halfway down a body, or a claim about data that has just been
// read.

// Stop unless `held`. The message says what was expected, and it should say the expectation rather
// than the symptom: `assert_that(total == sum, "the ledger balances")` beats `"bad total"`.
//
// **It returns `Int` rather than nothing** — Burxt has no void — and the return is ignorable, so
// `assert_that(...)` reads as a statement. The value is always 0; there is no other value it could
// have, because the alternative to returning is not returning.
function assert_that(held: Bool, why: String) -> Int {
    if held {
        return 0;
    }
    return panic("assertion failed: " + why);
}

// Stop, saying why. The one the other three are written in terms of.
//
// The `return 0` after `exit(70)` is unreachable and the compiler requires it anyway: `exit` is a
// statement rather than an expression — `return exit(70);` is refused, because a call that ends the
// process has no value to give — and every path out of a function that promises an `Int` has to
// produce one. Left as a bare `0` rather than dressed up, because a reader should be able to see in
// one line that nothing here is doing anything clever.
function panic(why: String) -> Int {
    print_error("burxt panic: " + why);
    exit(70);
    return 0;
}

// A path that is not written yet. `return todo();` type-checks anywhere an `Int` is wanted, and
// stops loudly the first time anything reaches it.
//
// **Loud is the whole point, and it is what distinguishes this from the alternatives.** A stub that
// returns `0`, or an empty String, or an empty array, is a placeholder that PASSES — it will be
// discovered by a wrong number in a report six weeks later rather than by the first test that
// touches it. This one cannot be discovered late.
//
// No message argument, because the useful information is the file and line, and the message would
// only ever restate the function's own name.
function todo() -> Int {
    return panic("not written yet");
}

// A branch the program's own logic says cannot happen. Distinct from `todo()` in what it CLAIMS:
// `todo()` says "I have not written this"; `unreachable(why)` says "I have proved nothing can get
// here", and `why` is where that proof goes — `unreachable("the lexer only emits these four kinds")`
// tells whoever arrives which belief turned out to be false.
//
// The commonest honest use is the arm after an exhaustive test that the type system cannot see is
// exhaustive. If a `match` covers it, use the `match`: it is checked, and this is not.
function unreachable(why: String) -> Int {
    return panic("reached a place the program says is unreachable: " + why);
}

// ---- What is deliberately absent -------------------------------------------
//
// There is no `unwrap`, for the reason lib/option.bx gives.
//
// ---- `?` ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//     function halve_then_double(a: Int, b: Int) -> Result<Int, String> {
//         let n: Int = divide(a, b)?;      // the value, or return the error
//         return Result.Ok(n * 2);
//     }
//
// (An earlier version of this comment used a `parse_int` that does not exist. The real pair is
// `string_to_int(text, fallback)` and `string_parse_int(text) -> Option<Int>` in lib/string.bx,
// and NEITHER answers a Result — parsing a number is too ordinary to charge a `?` for. So it
// was the wrong function to illustrate `?` with, three times over now. A comment is
// documentation and rots the same way.)
//
// `?` yields the success value, or returns the failure from the enclosing function
// immediately. It recognises a failure by the VARIANT name — `Error` here, `None` in
// lib/option.bx — never by the enum's own name, which is what lets these two be
// library types rather than built-ins.
//
// It does NOT convert between error types. If the callee fails with a String and you
// fail with something else, write the `match`: somebody has to decide what the
// caller's failure means, and that decision does not belong to an operator. See
// spec/1.0/M8-ERRORS.md §1a for the reasoning, and the trigger that would add a
// conversion trait.
